<!-- ehos_app:architecture -->
`ehos_app`, the template the application was generated from, writes this section about what every
EHOS application shares, and an upgrade of the template rewrites it. What is this application's
own goes below it.

## The application on one page

One Phoenix application, with one database schema of its own, which EHOS deploys and gives
everything it needs at start.

```text
  person, application or agent, with a credential EHOS issued
          │ HTTPS, port <%= @web_port %>
          ▼
  <%= @web %>.Router ── pipeline :commands ── EhOsContract.Auth.Plug ── EhOsContract.Plug
                                                                          │ dispatch
                                                                          ▼
  lib/<%= @app %>/contracts/  ──  the implementation decides  ──  event log (its own schema)
                                                                          │
                                         reactions: read models, policies, effects (Oban jobs)

  a person's browser ── /auth/ehos ── EhOsContract.Auth.SignIn.Router ── signs in at EHOS
          │ pages that need a person<%= if @access_page do %>, /access<% end %>: pipeline :signed_in ── EhOsContract.Auth.Session

  EHOS ── probes ──▶ <%= @web %>.Health, port <%= @health_port %>: /health/live, /health/ready
```

- **The domain**, `lib/<%= @app %>/domain.ex` (`use EhOsContract.Domain`), holds the words, the
  contexts and the actors. Its `name:` is the application's name in EHOS, and every capability
  and role is named after it.
- **A contract** per business process, in `lib/<%= @app %>/contracts/`, declares the commands, the
  events they may emit, the invariants, the queries and the promises. Its implementation is a
  module of the application's that `use`s the contract.
- **A command** arrives at `/api/commands/<domain>.<command>` with a credential, which
  `EhOsContract.Auth.Plug` verifies against EHOS's keys, held in the application's own database.
  The engine refuses an actor the contract does not name, has the implementation decide, and
  appends the events it emits to the event log, in one transaction.
- **A person** signs in through EHOS, never here: `/auth/ehos/sign-in` sends the browser to EHOS,
  which returns it to `/auth/ehos/callback`. The session is a row of the application's database,
  with EHOS's tokens encrypted, and the cookie names it. A page behind the `:signed_in` pipeline
  has the person, and `EhOsContract.Auth.Session.actor/1` gives the actor their credential is.
  A person no role of the application is bound to is refused at EHOS's sign-in, and never
  arrives.<%= if @access_page do %> `/access` is such a page: it shows the person their subject, the opaque
  identifier EHOS gives them, and which of the capabilities the contracts declare their roles
  grant. The home page, `/`, is public and links to it.<% end %>
- **The web layer** is `lib/<%= @app %>_web/`, whose own rules are in its `AGENTS.md`.

## What starts, in what order

The order in `lib/<%= @app %>/application.ex` is a contract, not a preference:

1. **The repo**, then **Oban**: the engine writes through the one and runs its reactions on the
   other.
2. **`EhOsContract`**, which records at boot which version of the contracts is deployed.
3. **`EhOsContract.Auth`**, the key store: it loads EHOS's keys from its table, then from EHOS.
4. **`<%= @web %>.Health`**, ready once the application holds a key, since without one it refuses
   every credential.
5. **`<%= @module %>.SignIn`**, the client people sign in with. It starts only where sign-in is
   configured, which is in a release on EHOS.
6. **The endpoint**, last, so nothing is served before the rest is up.

## What EHOS gives it

In production the application is configured by what EHOS gives it at start, read in
`config/runtime.exs`, and by nothing else:

| Variable | What it is |
| --- | --- |
| `EHOS_ISSUER`, `EHOS_AUDIENCE` | Who issues the credentials the application accepts, and for which `<application>/<environment>` |
| `EHOS_CA_FILE` | The authority that certifies EHOS's address, when the installation has its own |
| `DATABASE_URL`, `DATABASE_SCHEMA`, `DATABASE_CA_CERT_FILE` | The database: the connection, the schema that is its `search_path` and Oban's prefix, and the authority its certificate is checked against |
| `EHOS_CLIENT_ID`, `EHOS_CLIENT_KEY_FILE`, `EHOS_SIGN_IN_APPLICATIONS`, `EHOS_SIGN_IN_ABSOLUTE` | Signing people in, given because `ehos.yaml` declares `sign_in` |
| `PHX_HOST`, `PHX_SERVER`, `SECRET_KEY_BASE` | As Phoenix reads them |
| `EHOS_EGRESS_PROXY`, `HTTPS_PROXY`, `NO_PROXY` | The application's proxy to the hosts outside the installation it names, and the names it reaches directly; given only when `ehos.yaml` names such a host |

EHOS gives no port: the endpoints listen on <%= @web_port %> and <%= @health_port %>, the ports
`ehos.yaml` declares, and a change to one is a change to both. Every table is in the schema EHOS
gives the application. At run time its role may read and write there; only the migrations, run as
the schema's owner before the release starts, change a table.

## Reaching a host outside the installation

The application reaches nothing outside the installation but the hosts its `ehos.yaml` names,
and those only through a proxy of its own, which EHOS gives it as `EHOS_EGRESS_PROXY`, from EHOS
0.19.0. A host is named under `needs.egress`, with its port, 443 unless said, in `schema:
ehos.yaml/v5`, whose schema comment then points at
`deps/eh_os_contract_auth/packages/eh_os_contract_auth/priv/schemas/ehos.yaml/v5.json`. The proxy
admits TLS alone, to those names alone, checked in the request and in the TLS handshake; every
other connection out is refused. Naming a host is part of what the application may do: a
deployment or a promotion that adds, removes or changes one needs the right to register the
application, and only a deployment changes the hosts in force.

Req reads none of these variables. The client that calls such a host is given the proxy with
`EhOsContract.Auth.Egress.attach/1`, from `eh_os_contract_auth`, which leaves a host `NO_PROXY`
names, such as EHOS or another application, direct, and a plain `http` URL too, where it is
refused, and does nothing where `EHOS_EGRESS_PROXY` is not set:

```elixir
Req.new(base_url: "https://registry.example.org")
|> EhOsContract.Auth.Egress.attach()
|> Req.get!(url: "/companies/123")
```

TLS is verified against the host the request names, through the proxy as without it. The plugin
refuses a client with a Finch pool of its own, which Req cannot give a proxy, and one that speaks
HTTP/2 alone. A client that is not Req reads `HTTPS_PROXY` and `NO_PROXY` itself, or is given
`EHOS_EGRESS_PROXY` as an HTTP proxy it opens a `CONNECT` tunnel through, for `https` URLs alone
and never for a host `NO_PROXY` names; a client that ignores them gets a refused connection.

## Assets and the design system

`assets/` is Phoenix's asset pipeline: `app.css` and `app.js`, built by Tailwind and esbuild,
which Mix installs and runs (`mix assets.build`), into `priv/static/`.
<%= if @design_system_declined do %>The application declined the EmpoweredHouse design system, and its look is its own.<% else %>The EmpoweredHouse design system is not copied there: it is the `ehos_design` dependency, whose
fonts the endpoint serves from `/ehos_design/fonts/`. <%= if @design_system_imported do %>`assets/css/app.css` imports it right
after Tailwind, and `assets/js/app.js` registers its components' hooks.
<% else %>`assets/css/app.css` does
not import it yet: the installer found no line there starting `@import "tailwindcss"` to put the
import after. `<%= @design_system %>/README.md` says what to add to `app.css`,
and which hooks to register in `assets/js/app.js`.
<% end %>A token is overridden at the end of `app.css`, and a later version arrives by moving the
dependency.<% end %>

## The image and the manifest

On `main`, CI builds the release image for amd64 and arm64, pushes it to `ghcr.io` as one index
named by its digest, and publishes beside it the manifest `mix eh_os_contract.manifest` writes from
`ehos.yaml` and the contracts. EHOS deploys the image by that digest, never by a tag, and reads the
application's capabilities, roles and needs from the manifest. The release writes under `/tmp`
alone and starts no distribution (`rel/env.sh.eex`), since EHOS runs it with a read-only
filesystem.

## EHOS's decisions it is built on

The argument for each is in EHOS's repository.

- **`ehos.yaml` is EHOS's format**, a versioned JSON Schema
  ([D-51](https://github.com/EmpoweredHouse/eh-os/blob/main/docs/adr/0051-ehos-yaml-is-defined-by-ehos.md)):
  it says what the application needs, never how it is provided.
- **EHOS's operator deploys the application** into a namespace per environment, by digest, and
  routes to it ([D-55](https://github.com/EmpoweredHouse/eh-os/blob/main/docs/adr/0055-operator-realises-environments-and-deployments.md)).
  Nothing of EHOS is on the path of a request, so the application keeps serving while EHOS is
  stopped.
- **Everything is given at start**, as variables and mounted files with fixed names
  ([D-56](https://github.com/EmpoweredHouse/eh-os/blob/main/docs/adr/0056-what-a-workload-is-given-at-start.md)),
  and nothing is fetched from EHOS afterwards but its keys.
- **People sign in at EHOS**, and an application has no login of its own (D-64).
- **A host outside the installation is reached only by naming it**, through a proxy of the
  application's own ([D-135](https://github.com/EmpoweredHouse/eh-os/blob/main/docs/adr/0135-an-application-reaches-a-host-outside-the-installation-only-by-naming-it-in-ehos-yaml-through-a-proxy-of-its-own-that-checks-the-name-in-the-tls-handshake.md)).
